  "summary": "A traditional Fijian dwelling built with natural materials thatched together to form conical roofs.",
  "description": "Features distinctive conical thatched roofs and woven bamboo walls, designed for tropical climate ventilation. The architecture reflects Fijian social hierarchy, with larger structures for chiefs and smaller ones for commoners.",
  "culturalContext": "The Fijian bure — traditional thatched dwellings of the village compound — rise from coral-lime or earth floors with hardwood posts, woven (voi) bamboo walls, and a tall thatched (soga) hipped roof ridge-lined like a hull: chiefly bure kalou bore taller roof spikes and sacred thresholds. Modern resort \"bures\" quote the form in luxury villas; chiefly villages (Navesau, Navala) preserve authentic construction, and bure-building ceremonies remain fealty events in Fijian chiefly culture.",
